{{How a Bicycle Derailleur Moves the Chain Between Gears}}

A derailleur shifts the chain by pushing it sideways while the chain is moving. When you click the shifter, it changes tension on a **cable**. In a rear derailleur, that cable pulls against a spring-loaded parallelogram mechanism. This parallelogram moves the derailleur cage—and its two jockey pulleys—either inward or outward.

Because the chain is routed through the cage, the cage guides the chain sideways toward a different **sprocket** on the rear cassette. As you pedal, the chain is forced to climb or drop from one sprocket to the next. The spring returns the derailleur when cable tension is released. A front derailleur works similarly, using the cable to move a cage side to side so the chain moves between chainrings. The chain must be pedaled during the shift for the derailleur to actually change gears.

